        content: 'Most teams end up choosing between three approaches, and they are not mutually exclusive across a whole organization, just for a given phone number. The first is provisioning a new number directly through your voice AI platform. This is the fastest path: you pick a country and area code, the platform assigns you a live number in minutes, and you point your agent at it. It works well when you are launching a new campaign, a new line of business, or a number that customers have not seen before, so there is no continuity to protect. The second is porting an existing business number, the one already printed on your website, your ads, and your customers\' saved contacts, over to the platform. Porting is a formal telecom process rather than an instant switch: your new provider requests the number from your current carrier, and there is typically a short window, often a few hours to a couple of days, where the number is in transition and calls need careful handling to avoid gaps. It is the right call when the number itself carries business value you cannot afford to lose. The third is bring-your-own-carrier, or BYOC, using a SIP trunk to connect telephony infrastructure you already run through providers like Twilio, Vonage, or a traditional carrier. This gives you the most control over routing, compliance, and existing integrations, but it also means you are managing the SIP configuration yourself rather than letting the platform handle number provisioning end to end.',
