        content: 'Across the voice AI market, pricing tends to fall into one of four structural patterns. The first is pure usage-based billing, where you pay a flat rate per minute of talk time and nothing else. The second is a hybrid model: a fixed monthly platform or subscription fee combined with a discounted per-minute rate, so heavier usage earns a lower marginal cost in exchange for a fixed base fee. The third is unbundled component pricing, where the voice engine, the underlying LLM, speech-to-text, text-to-speech, and telephony (the phone line itself) are billed as separate line items rather than one blended rate, which can make the "sticker price" look lower until you add everything up. The fourth is custom or enterprise pricing, reserved for higher volume or specialized needs like healthcare compliance, and typically requires a sales conversation rather than a published rate card. Most self-serve platforms mix elements of the first three; enterprise deals usually layer the fourth on top.',
