        content: 'If voice AI has a genuine foothold anywhere in 2026, it is in the contact center, and that is not an accident. Contact centers already run on scripted, measurable, high-volume interactions, exactly the conditions where an AI agent can be evaluated against a clear baseline: average handle time, cost per call, containment rate. That measurability is what let Gartner make a specific numerical prediction about contact center automation in the first place, something that would be much harder to forecast for, say, ad hoc voice interactions across an entire company. Contact centers also tend to have the call volume that justifies the engineering investment: a business fielding a few dozen calls a week has little reason to build a voice AI deployment, while one fielding thousands has a clear cost case. That is why the current state of voice AI looks less like a general-purpose voice layer replacing phone calls everywhere and more like a steadily growing share of a specific, high-volume, already-measured use case.',
