        content: 'The per-minute rate a vendor advertises is designed to be comparable and cheap-looking, and it is frequently not the number that ends up on your invoice. Common additions include a monthly platform or subscription fee on top of usage, per-seat charges if multiple team members need access to call logs or configuration, phone number rental fees, charges for outbound versus inbound minutes that differ from the advertised rate, and overage pricing once you exceed a bundled minute allotment. None of these are necessarily unreasonable, but they are easy to miss when comparing a quoted per-minute price across vendors. The concrete step is to ask each vendor for a full monthly cost estimate based on your actual expected call volume, itemized by category, not just the per-minute rate, and to get that estimate in writing before you sign. It is also worth asking what happens at the edges: what is the overage rate once you exceed your plan, is there a minimum monthly commitment, and what does it cost to cancel or move providers if the platform does not work out. A vendor confident in their pricing will answer all of this without friction.',
